  {
    "number": 106213,
    "title": "Refactor XLA Profiler to be Framework-Agnostic via Shared C-State",
    "user": "copybara-service[bot]",
    "created_at": "2025-12-14T13:17:36",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-19T15:25:04",
    "body": "Refactor XLA Profiler to be Framework-Agnostic via Shared C-State\n\nThis CL refactors the XLA profiler to eliminate its direct C++ dependency on TensorFlow's Python modules. This change resolves deadlocks that occurred in non-TensorFlow environments (e.g., JAX-only) and creates a more robust, high-performance, and architecturally clean solution.\n\n## Problem\nPreviously, the C++ PythonHookContext would directly import the tensorflow.python.profiler.trace Python module to set an enabled flag. This operation was performed while the C++ thread already held Python's Global Interpreter Lock (GIL).\n\nThis caused several critical issues:\n\n* GIL Deadlocks: If the import failed (e.g., because TensorFlow was not installed), pybind11's exception handler would attempt to re-acquire the GIL. This caused the thread to deadlock by waiting for a lock it already held.\n* Fragile Architecture: This made the profiler fragile and unusable outside of a full TensorFlow environment, affecting JAX-only users.\n* Tight Coupling: It created an architecturally unsound dependency from a low-level C++ library to a high-level Python module.\n\n## Solution\nThis change decouples the C++ profiler from Python's module structure by using a shared C++ memory location as the single source of truth for the profiler's state.\n\n1. Shared C++ State (traceme_state):\n\n    * A new, minimal cc_library     (//third_party/tensorflow/compiler/xla/python/profiler/internal:traceme_state) is introduced.\n    * It owns a global std::atomic<bool> that serves as the definitive \"TraceMe enabled\" state.\n    * The C++ PythonHookContext now writes directly to this C++ variable, removing all Python module interactions.\n\n2. High-Performance Python Access via ctypes:\n\n   * The _pywrap_traceme pybind extension is updated with a get_traceme_enabled_addr() function that returns the raw memory address of the C++ atomic boolean.\n   * In Python, trace.py calls this function once on import and uses the ctypes library to create a direct pointer to this memory address.\nThis allows Python code to check the trace.enabled status with the performance of a direct memory read, completely avoiding any pybind function call overhead.\n\n## Benefits\n* Fixes Deadlocks: By removing the cross-language import, the GIL deadlock issue is completely resolved.\n* Decouples Code: The profiler's C++ core is now truly framework-agnostic.\nHigh Performance: The critical trace.enabled check in Python remains a fast, overhead-free memory access.\n* Clean Architecture: The dependency is now unidirectional and logical—Python reads from a C++ utility, not the other way around.\n",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/pull/106213",
